From: jongkyung.woo <jkwoo@gooroom.kr>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the signed grub.cfg of gooroom-boot-protector

gooroom-boot-protector ships grub.cfg with a detached signature made at
package build time, and the stand-alone grub (embedded pubkey) refuses to
load a grub.cfg whose signature does not match. update-grub, the kernel
postinst hook and grub package postinsts all call grub-mkconfig, which
overwrote the signed file so the next boot stopped at the trusted boot
failure screen. When /boot/grub/grub.cfg has a .sig and the protector's
signed files are installed, write the generated config to
grub.cfg.gbp-generated instead of overwriting the signed one.
---
Index: wt-gbp-5.0/util/grub-mkconfig.in
===================================================================
--- wt-gbp-5.0.orig/util/grub-mkconfig.in
+++ wt-gbp-5.0/util/grub-mkconfig.in
@@ -317,7 +317,20 @@ and /etc/grub.d/* files or please file a
     # none of the children aborted with error, install the new grub.cfg
     oldumask=$(umask)
     umask 077
-    cat ${grub_cfg}.new > ${grub_cfg}
+    # gooroom grub: gooroom-boot-protector installs grub.cfg together with a
+    # detached signature (grub.cfg.sig) made at package build time, and the
+    # stand-alone grub refuses to load a grub.cfg whose signature does not match.
+    # Overwriting it here would stop the next boot at the trusted boot failure
+    # screen, so keep the signed file and leave the generated one beside it.
+    gbp_dir=/usr/share/gooroom/security/gooroom-boot-protector
+    if [ "$(readlink -f "${grub_cfg}")" = "/boot/grub/grub.cfg" ] && \
+       [ -f "${grub_cfg}.sig" ] && \
+       { [ -f "${gbp_dir}/grub.cfg.sig" ] || [ -f "${gbp_dir}/grub.cfg.ima.sig" ]; }; then
+      cat ${grub_cfg}.new > ${grub_cfg}.gbp-generated
+      gettext_printf "Kept the signed %s of gooroom-boot-protector; the generated config is in %s\n" "${grub_cfg}" "${grub_cfg}.gbp-generated" >&2
+    else
+      cat ${grub_cfg}.new > ${grub_cfg}
+    fi
     umask $oldumask
     rm -f ${grub_cfg}.new
   fi
